# LiveSelect cheatsheet

## Label and validation errors

Keep the label and errors alongside the field in your application's template. The text input ID is the form field ID followed by `_text_input`. This example uses the application's existing `translate_error/1` function:

```heex
<label for={"#{@form[:city].id}_text_input"}>City</label>
<.live_select field={@form[:city]} />
<div :if={used_input?(@form[:city])}>
  <p :for={error <- @form[:city].errors}>{translate_error(error)}</p>
</div>
```

When the field belongs to a LiveComponent, add `phx-target={@myself}` to LiveSelect so its events reach that component. Use your application's shared input and error classes to match the rest of its form controls. `used_input?/1` is the [LiveView API for filtering errors on unused fields](https://hexdocs.pm/phoenix_live_view/Phoenix.Component.html#used_input?/1).

## Search after typing

Set `update_min_len={1}` to search only when there is text. Clear the available options whenever the input receives focus:

```heex
<.live_select field={@form[:locations]} update_min_len={1} phx-focus="clear-options" />
```

```elixir
@impl true
def handle_event("live_select_change", %{"id" => id, "text" => text}, socket) do
  query = String.downcase(text)

  options =
    retrieve_locations()
    |> Enum.filter(fn location -> String.contains?(String.downcase(location), query) end)

  send_update(LiveSelect.Component, options: options, id: id)

  {:noreply, socket}
end

@impl true
def handle_event("clear-options", %{"id" => id}, socket) do
  send_update(LiveSelect.Component, options: [], id: id)

  {:noreply, socket}
end
```

`retrieve_locations/0` represents the application's existing source of location names. For a database-backed search, let the application's query filter the available options instead.

## Default options for an empty search

Set `update_min_len={0}` and initialise the field with the options to show before the user enters text:

```heex
<.live_select
  field={@form[:locations]}
  update_min_len={0}
  phx-focus="default-options"
  options={@default_locations}
/>
```

```elixir
@impl true
def mount(_params, _session, socket) do
  {:ok, assign(socket, default_locations: ["Berlin", "London", "New York"])}
end

@impl true
def handle_event("live_select_change", %{"id" => id, "text" => text}, socket) do
  options =
    if text == "" do
      socket.assigns.default_locations
    else
      query = String.downcase(text)

      retrieve_locations()
      |> Enum.filter(fn location -> String.contains?(String.downcase(location), query) end)
    end

  send_update(LiveSelect.Component, options: options, id: id)

  {:noreply, socket}
end

@impl true
def handle_event("default-options", %{"id" => id}, socket) do
  send_update(LiveSelect.Component, options: socket.assigns.default_locations, id: id)

  {:noreply, socket}
end
```

Merge the `default_locations` assign into your existing `mount/3` callback, which also prepares the form.

## Open the dropdown above the field

For Tailwind:

```heex
<.live_select
  field={@form[:city]}
  dropdown_extra_class="!top-full bottom-full"
/>
```

For daisyUI:

```heex
<.live_select
  field={@form[:city]}
  style={:daisyui}
  container_extra_class="dropdown-top"
/>
```

The leading `!` removes a default class in LiveSelect's extra-class options.

## Display tags underneath the input

Use a single-column grid with the text input first and tags last:

```heex
<.live_select
  field={@form[:cities]}
  mode={:tags}
  container_extra_class="grid grid-cols-[1fr]"
  tags_container_extra_class="order-last"
/>
```

The dropdown remains positioned below the component. To place it above the component, combine this with the previous recipe.

## Limit the dropdown height

```heex
<.live_select
  field={@form[:city]}
  dropdown_extra_class="max-h-60 overflow-y-auto"
/>
```

## Use different selected labels

Use `selected_label` to show a shorter label in single mode:

```heex
<.live_select
  field={@form[:country]}
  options={[%{label: "+60 Malaysia (MY)", selected_label: "+60", value: "MY"}]}
/>
```

The dropdown shows `+60 Malaysia (MY)`, the selected input shows `+60`, and the submitted value is `MY`. Use `tag_label` for the corresponding label in `:tags` and `:quick_tags` modes.
